ass: NEON bitmap engine for 32-bit ARM

libass has assembly for x86 and for AArch64. A 32-bit ARM build, which is
what Fire TV sticks and many Android TV devices run whatever their cores
are, rendered with the C functions, and blur and rasterizer are 70% of
the time a typeset episode takes there.

libass/arm/ holds NEON versions of them, written with intrinsics so that
they build with the C compiler alone: the blur stripes (unpack, pack,
shrink, expand and the sixteen blur kernels), the rasterizer tiles (solid,
halfplane, generic), the bitmap blending (add, imul, mul) and be_blur.
merge_tile stays C: the compiler makes the same of it. The engine is
picked where the compiler targets NEON (__ARM_NEON; Android's armeabi-v7a
always does), there is no runtime detection to get wrong.

Every function gives the bytes its C version gives, within the value
ranges the engine works in (the AArch64 assembly relies on the same ones).
libass' checkasm compares them, and now builds for 32-bit ARM (it needs no
assembly there: an AAPCS call checks nothing). Run on the device:

    checkasm: all 32 tests passed       (eleven seeds, Fire TV Stick 4K Max)

C against NEON on that device's Cortex-A55, checkasm --bench: stripe pack
5.6x, unpack 3.9x, shrink 4x and 9x, expand 6x and 12x, the blur kernels
4.9x (radius 1) to 2.5x (radius 8), be_blur 3.9x, fill_solid 2.1x and
3.2x, fill_halfplane 2x, fill_generic 1.4x and 1.5x (half of it is scalar
work per segment), add_bitmaps 2.4x, imul and mul_bitmaps 1.15x (the
compiler vectorizes the C ones).

The heaviest 160 s of a typeset episode on that device, one thread, built
without and with these functions: 15.4 s and 10.3 s of rendering, 38 and
20 frames that take longer than their duration, the same bytes.

